ART AVENUE — STORAGE / LEATHER & KILIM PRODUCT SCHEMA TEMPLATES

Companion to:
  - snippets/aa-chair-schema-templates.txt.liquid   (chair/sofa configurator schemas)
  - snippets/aa-alt-text-convention.txt.liquid      (image alt-text standard)
  - snippets/aa-material-notes-library.txt.liquid    (material trust notes)

=========================================================================
GOVERNING RULE — LEATHER & KILIM ARE ALWAYS SEPARATE PRODUCTS
=========================================================================
Leather and kilim are NEVER a construction toggle on one product. Each is its
own product / PDP. Both are FIXED PRICE per size, with free line-item property
pickers (no priced finish axis).

  LEATHER product: full Leather Colour picker + Metal Finish.
  KILIM product:  Kilim shown as a FIXED LABEL (with trust note) + a LIMITED
                  Leather Trim picker + Metal Finish.

Wherever kilim is the main top fabric on ANY furniture category, the PDP shows:
one fixed Kilim + leather trim (limited) + metal finish. Leather-only pieces
show the full leather colour range + metal finish.

=========================================================================
LEATHER IS TWO SEPARATE LIBRARIES, SPLIT BY USE (category value)
=========================================================================
There are TWO leather swatch libraries, distinguished only by the swatch
'category' field. The engine filters by category, so a product simply points at
whichever library fits — no code change, ever.

  UPHOLSTERY LEATHER  — category 'Leather'           (soft seating hides)
      Used by: sofas, lounge chairs, bed headboards, ottomans, benches.
  FURNITURE LEATHER   — category 'Furniture Leather'  (hard-furniture leather)
      Used by: trunks, coffee tables, chests of drawers, wardrobes, consoles.

They are DIFFERENT colour ranges (10 each), same ₹2,000 swatch price. A product
points its leather picker/tier_filter at ONE category and lists that library's
IDs in aav.swatches. Never mix the two on one product.
  - Upholstery product  -> category 'Leather'
  - Non-upholstery piece -> category 'Furniture Leather'
(Coffee tables may instead use the table-specific 'Table Leather' set — that is a
per-category choice; the point is the engine keys entirely off 'category'.)

=========================================================================
ENGINE MAPPING (why the JSON looks like a 'grid')
=========================================================================
Both patterns run on the GRID engine. The CONSTRUCTION axis (Leather | Kilim)
has exactly ONE value per product, so the engine renders it as a static fixed
label (mat_count==1 path) — no toggle. Pickers ride on top via cfg.pickers[].

The SIZE axis may be single OR multiple:
  - Single size  -> one variant, size button hidden, dims shown from the one key.
  - Multiple sizes (e.g. Square / Rectangular) -> one variant PER size, size
    buttons shown, price + dims switch per size automatically.
All variants: inventoryPolicy CONTINUE (made to order).

The leather picker uses branch_by_axis:true so it filters via tier_filters keyed
by the single Construction value. include_names limits which swatches show (kilim).

=========================================================================
*** DIMENSIONS RULE — READ THIS, IT IS THE #1 GOTCHA ***
=========================================================================
The grid engine reads dimensions from the aav.size_dimensions metafield, KEYED
BY THE EXACT SIZE-BUTTON VALUE (case + spelling must match the option value).
cfg.unit_map is the fallback and must carry the SAME keys.
The dim_w / dim_d / dim_h number fields are NOT read by the grid engine.

Whenever you ADD, RENAME, or REMOVE a size value you MUST update BOTH
aav.size_dimensions and cfg.unit_map to have one matching key per size value.
If a size button has no matching key, the dimensions box renders EMPTY.

Example — a product with sizes [Square, Rectangular]:
  aav.size_dimensions =
    {"Square":{"cm":"45 × 45 × 45 cm","in":"17.7 × 17.7 × 17.7 in"},
     "Rectangular":{"cm":"90 × 45 × 45 cm","in":"35.4 × 17.7 × 17.7 in"}}
  cfg.unit_map =
    {"Square":"45 × 45 × 45 cm","Rectangular":"90 × 45 × 45 cm"}
  (cm->in: 45->17.7, 90->35.4, 60->23.6, 120->47.2, 75->29.5, 80->31.5)

=========================================================================
*** SWATCH-LIBRARY PROPAGATION RULE — #2 GOTCHA ***
=========================================================================
Products reference swatches BY METAOBJECT ID in aav.swatches. Adding a new
colour to a shared library (e.g. a new Leather swatch metaobject) does NOT
auto-appear on any product — each product only shows the IDs listed in its OWN
aav.swatches. A library addition reaches a PDP only when its ID is appended to
that product's aav.swatches.

SYMPTOM when this is missed: the picker shows FEWER swatches than the library
(e.g. a leather grid that stops at 6 when the library has 10). This looks like a
UI/scroll bug but is actually a DATA gap — the missing colours were never linked.

WHENEVER YOU ADD A SWATCH TO A SHARED LIBRARY:
  1. Decide every product that should carry the new colour.
  2. Append the new metaobject ID to each of those products' aav.swatches list
     (preserve existing order; add new IDs after the existing same-category block).
  3. Re-check any tier_filters.include_names — a name listed there must also be
     present in aav.swatches or it won't resolve.

Each leather library is 10 IDs (see the two LIBRARY sections below). Any leather
product that should show its full range must list all 10 of the RIGHT library.
Intentionally limited sets (e.g. kilim trim = Ebony + Chestnut only) are the
deliberate exception — short by design, not a gap.

AUDIT QUERY (run after any library change): fetch products with
`metafield:aav.swatches`, expand each swatches reference's name+category, and
compare each product's leather count against the canonical 10 for ITS library
('Leather' for upholstery pieces, 'Furniture Leather' for hard furniture).
Anything short (other than the kilim-trim products) needs the missing IDs appended.

